**Theorem 1.2 (Faithful irreducible rigidity over $\mathbb Q_2$).** For every finite Galois extension $L/\mathbb Q_2$, put $G=\operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb Q_2)$. Let $\rho_1,\rho_2:G\to PGL_3(\mathbb C)$ be injective homomorphisms. If $\rho_1$ is projectively irreducible and the two maps are element-conjugate, there exists one $h\in PGL_3(\mathbb C)$ such that

$$
\rho_2(g)=h\rho_1(g)h^{-1}\qquad\text{for every }g\in G.
$$

Proof. All subgroup restrictions and conjugations below retain the original source elements. No homomorphic lift on all of $G$ is assumed.

### 1.1. Ramification and subgroup lifts

Let $I$ and $P$ be inertia and wild inertia. The residue action identifies $G/I$ with a cyclic group. For a uniformizer $\varpi$ of $L$, the map

$$
I\longrightarrow k_L^\times,\qquad
\nu\longmapsto\overline{\nu(\varpi)/\varpi}
$$

is a homomorphism with kernel $P$. Thus $I/P$ is cyclic of odd order. The higher ramification filtration of $P$ terminates; its successive quotients embed in the additive group of $k_L$ by the leading coefficient of $\nu(\varpi)-\varpi$. Consequently $P$ is a $2$-group. A lift $F\in G$ of arithmetic residue Frobenius acts on $I/P$ by squaring, since its residue action is $x\mapsto x^2$. These valuation descriptions apply in residue characteristic two.

For any finite subgroup $H\subset PGL_3(\mathbb C)$ its inverse image $\widetilde H$ in $SL_3(\mathbb C)$ is finite of order $3|H|$ and has the same invariant subspaces as the full inverse image in $GL_3(\mathbb C)$. If $H$ is a $2$-group, a Sylow $2$-subgroup $Q$ maps isomorphically onto $H$. Moreover $\widetilde H=\mu_3Q$; centrality of $\mu_3$ makes $Q$ normal, hence the unique Sylow $2$-subgroup. This gives a canonical lift on $H$, compatible with every conjugation normalizing $H$.

Suppose first that $P\ne1$. Apply this lift to $\rho_i(P)$, obtaining ordinary representations $A_i:P\to SL_3(\mathbb C)$. The irreducible finite group $\widetilde{\rho_1(G)}$ permutes the $P$-isotypic components transitively: otherwise a proper orbit sum is invariant. All components therefore have the same dimension and multiplicity. Irreducible degrees of a finite $2$-group are powers of two, by the standard degree-divisibility theorem ([Teleman, §13, Theorem 13.6](https://math.berkeley.edu/~teleman/math/RepThry.pdf); see also the [repository source note](../../../Library/FiniteGroups/teleman2005representation.md)). Each common irreducible degree divides three, so is one. There are either one or three isotypic components. In the first case $A_1(P)$ is scalar; a scalar in $SL_3(\mathbb C)$ has order dividing three, so its $2$-power order makes it trivial. Faithfulness excludes this. Hence there are three distinct character lines, transitively permuted by $G$.

At $p\in P$, projective conjugacy of $\rho_1(p)$ and $\rho_2(p)$ gives

$$
A_2(p)=\zeta H A_1(p)H^{-1},\qquad\zeta\in\mu_3.
$$

Both sides without $\zeta$ have order dividing $\operatorname{ord}(p)$, so $\zeta^{\operatorname{ord}(p)}=1$ and $\zeta=1$. Thus the ordinary characters of $A_1,A_2$ agree. Ordinary finite-group character equivalence supplies one conjugator aligning them on all of $P$; this is the character interface of [Fang–Han–Sun, Proposition 2.1](https://msp.org/pjm/2016/283-1/pjm-v283-n1-p05-s.pdf), also used in [the toral source-support proof, §§1 and 2.1](TORAL_CHARACTER_TRIPLE_SOURCE_SUPPORT.md#21-scalar-normalization-and-character-multiplicities). Canonical lifting implies that both images of each $g$ implement the same conjugation $p\mapsto gpg^{-1}$ on these aligned lifts. Distinct characters force the same permutation of the three lines. Both maps are therefore monomial with one common transitive permutation map $\pi:G\to S_3$.

If $P=1$, inertia is cyclic. Its inverse image in $SL_3(\mathbb C)$ is abelian, being generated by a lift of a generator and central scalars. Its weight spaces are permuted transitively by $\widetilde{\rho_1(G)}$. A single weight would make $\rho_1(I)$ trivial. Then faithfulness gives $I=1$, so $G$ is cyclic, contradicting projective irreducibility: a lift of a cyclic generator has an eigenline. There are consequently three distinct weight lines. Inertia fixes them individually, while the cyclic quotient $G/I$ acts transitively through $C_3$. The matrix for $F$ is a monomial three-cycle, whose cube is scalar. Faithfulness gives $F^3=1$ and $G/I=C_3$. The squaring action on $I$ now gives $x^8=x$, so nontrivial $I$ has order seven. Conjugacy at a generator aligns $\rho_2|_I$ with $\rho_1|_I$. The determinant-one lift of each value having order dividing seven is unique: two such lifts differ by $\mu_3$, and their seventh powers remove that ambiguity. Both maps consequently permute the three aligned weight lines in the same way. Again they are monomial with the same transitive $\pi$.

### 1.2. The diagonal kernel

Set $D=\ker\pi$. Faithfulness identifies $D$ with a finite subgroup of $T$, so $D$ is abelian. Also $P\subset D$. Since $\pi(I)$ is a normal cyclic odd-order subgroup of a transitive subgroup of $S_3$, it is either trivial or $C_3$.

If $\pi(I)=1$, cyclicity of $G/I$ forces $\pi(G)=C_3$. The Frobenius lift has three-cycle permutation, hence projective order three, so $F^3=1$ by faithfulness. Its image generates $G/I$, which therefore has order three, and $D=I$. Squaring on $I/P$ gives $|I/P|\mid7$. In particular $3\nmid|D|$.

If $\pi(I)=C_3$, choose $\tau\in I$ lifting a generator of $I/P$. Its three-cycle permutation gives $\tau^3=1$, so $|I/P|=3$. Frobenius inverts this quotient; hence $\pi(F)$ is a transposition, $\pi(G)=S_3$, and $D\cap I=P$. The group $D/P$ embeds in cyclic $G/I$, so all conjugation on it is trivial. Write $\sigma$ for the action of $\tau$ on $T$. Directly from the three cyclic coordinate equations,

$$
T^\sigma=\{[\operatorname{diag}(1,z,z^2)]:z^3=1\}.
$$

Let $D_2$ be the $2$-primary subgroup of $D$. The map $d\mapsto\sigma(d)d^{-1}$ is an endomorphism of $D_2$ with trivial kernel, hence is surjective. Its image lies in $P$, because conjugation is trivial modulo $P$. Since $P\subset D_2$, this proves $D_2=P$. The odd-primary subgroup $O$ of $D$ injects into $D/P$. Thus $\sigma$ fixes $O$, putting it inside $T^\sigma$. A transposition acts on this order-three subgroup by inversion. Frobenius also acts trivially on $O$ because it acts trivially modulo $P$. Therefore every element of $O$ equals its inverse, forcing $O=1$. This branch gives $D=P$.

In both branches $3\nmid|D|$, and the restriction of $\rho_1$ to $D$ has three distinct weight lines: those of $P$ if $P\ne1$, and those of $I=C_7$ otherwise.

### 1.3. Alignment and the torus cocycle

The required alignment on $D$ reuses determinant normalization and character multiplicities from [the toral proof, §2.1](TORAL_CHARACTER_TRIPLE_SOURCE_SUPPORT.md#21-scalar-normalization-and-character-multiplicities). Explicitly, let $N$ be the exponent of $D$ and choose $r$ with $3r+1\equiv0\pmod N$. Each toral map has a homomorphic diagonal lift $B=\operatorname{diag}(1,\alpha,\beta)$. Then

$$
A(d)=\det(B(d))^r B(d)
$$

is a determinant-one homomorphic lift with $A(d)^N=1$. The pointwise projective conjugacy scalar lies in both $\mu_3$ and $\mu_N$, hence is one. Ordinary character equivalence aligns the restrictions on all of $D$ by one conjugator. Uniqueness of this determinant-one, exponent-dividing-$N$ lift makes it compatible with conjugation by $G$. The three distinct weight lines consequently give identical permutations for both maps after alignment as well.

Now $\rho_2(g)=d(g)\rho_1(g)$ for a $T$-valued cocycle $d$, with

$$
d(gh)=d(g)\,g(d(h)).
$$

It is trivial on $D$. Since $D$ acts trivially on $T$, it descends to $K=G/D$, which is $C_3$ or $S_3$ with its permutation action. The following explicit calculation removes this cocycle.

For a three-cycle $\sigma$, the map $b\mapsto b/\sigma(b)$ is surjective on $T$. Indeed, represent a desired value by three nonzero coordinates, rescale them to product one, and solve the cyclic ratio equations. Conjugating $\rho_2$ by such a $b$ makes $d(\sigma)=1$. This finishes when $K=C_3$. Otherwise choose a transposition $s$ with $s\sigma s=\sigma^{-1}$ and put $a=d(s)$. The cocycle identities for $s^2=1$ and $s\sigma s\sigma=1$ give

$$
a\,s(a)=1=a\,s\sigma(a).
$$

Hence $\sigma(a)=a$, so $a\in T^\sigma$. On this order-three subgroup $s$ acts by inversion. Conjugation by $b\in T^\sigma$ leaves $d(\sigma)=1$ and replaces $a$ by $b^2a$. Squaring is bijective on this subgroup, so a choice of $b$ kills $a$. The cocycle is trivial on both generators, hence on $K$. Undoing the fixed conjugations gives the asserted single conjugator on the original $G$. ∎

